 -- How You Speak Is Who You Are -- Native Tongues -- How Language Divides Us -- Deep Talk -- Little Bigots? -- On the Basis of Speech -- A Linguistics Revolution -- Afterword -- Acknowledgments -- Notes -- Index -- About the Author -- Connect on Social Media -- Footnotes","note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Katherine D. Kinzler."},"Description based on print version record.","Part of the metadata in this record was created by AI, based on the text of the resource.","Description based on publisher supplied metadata and other sources."],"subject":{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Language and languages","Variation"]},"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"P120.V37 .K569 2020"},{"@edition":"23","@authority":"ddc","#text":"417.7"}],"relatedItem":[{"@type":"otherFormat","identifier":{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0-544-98655-5"}},{"@type":"otherFormat","identifier":{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0-358-56710-6"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/99154271402703941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9780544987425"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"054498742X"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"1346360406"}],"location":[null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/99154271402703941","processingDate":"2026-09-11T04:14Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"descriptionStandard":"rda pn","recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"MiAaPQ"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"201016"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20241126"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"99154271402703941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.8 using\n\t\t\t\t\tMARC21slim_MODS3-8_XSL2-0.xsl (Revision 2.79 2025/03/28)","languageOfCataloging":{"languageTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"}}}},{"titleInfo":{"title":"How you say it","subTitle":"why we judge others by the way they talk--and the costs of this hidden bias"},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Kinzler, Katherine D.","role":[{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}},{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"author."}}]},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"rdacontent","#text":"text"}],"originInfo":[{"place":{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"msu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"Mississippi"}]},"dateIssued":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2021"},"copyrightDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2020"},"edition":"First Mariner books edition.","issuance":"monographic"},{"@eventType":"publication","place":{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Boston :"}},"publisher":"Houghton Mifflin Harcourt,","dateIssued":[2021]}],"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":[{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},{"@type":"media","@authority":"rdamedia","#text":"unmediated"},{"@type":"carrier","@authority":"rdacarrier","#text":"volume"}],"extent":"xvi, 231 pages ; 21 cm"},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"\"We gravitate toward people like us; it's human nature. Race, class, and gender affect this social identity, but one overlooked factor can be even more powerful: the way we speak. As pioneering psychologist Katherine Kinzler reveals in How You Say It, that's because our speech largely reflects the voices we heard as children. We can change how we speak to some extent, whether by \"code-switching\" between dialects or learning a new language. But for the most part we are forever marked by our native tongue-and are hardwired to prejudge others by theirs, often with serious consequences. Your accent alone can determine the economic opportunity or discrimination you encounter in life, making speech one of the most urgent social-justice issues of our day. Ultimately, Kinzler shows, our linguistic differences can also be a force for good. For her research reveals that exposure to different languages is beneficial--a paradox that hints at the benefits we can reap from mastering this ancient source of tribalism\"--"},"tableOfContents":"Introduction: it's not what you say -- How you speak is who you are -- Native tongues -- How language divides us -- Deep talk -- Little bigots? -- On the basis of speech -- A linguistics revolution -- Afterword: it's not what you say.","note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Katherine D. Kinzler."},"\"Mariner Books.\"",{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references (pages 190-224) and index."}],"subject":[{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Sociolinguistics"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Language and languages","Variation"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Linguistic change","Social aspects"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Languages in contact"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Second language acquisition"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":["Language and languages","Variation"]},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Languages in contact"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":["Linguistic change","Social aspects"]},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Second language acquisition"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Sociolinguistics"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"P40.5.L54 K55 2021"},{"@edition":"23","@authority":"ddc","#text":"302.224"},{"@edition":"23","@authority":"ddc","#text":"306.44"}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0358567106"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9780358567103"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9780544986558"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0544986555"},{"@type":"isbn","@invalid":"yes","#text":"9780544987425"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"1192307819"}],"relatedItem":{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/99155860167303941/catalog"}},"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository"